    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2718f01712ec33d1b687d8dce149b0ec97bcd4c3c7beedd5ad9aa95a28b05dcb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The general L-function argument retains the zeta proof's architecture—completed-function zero representation, the same extremal minorant, and an explicit formula with bandwidth Delta—but replaces t by analytic conductor and the von Mangoldt weights by logarithmic-derivative coefficients of the L-function and its dual. Consequently the transfer cost is concentrated on the prime side: Ramanujan-strength coefficient bounds permit essentially the zeta bandwidth and leading constant, whereas absolute Euler-product convergence alone forces Delta to be about one third as large and produces the stated factor-three loss.",
